Whew, thought this was an issue in my library (`expo-speech-recognition`) and assumed it was a software update related to the android speech recognizer. 

Looks like you'll need to fetch the current permissions before requesting any, e.g. this is what I've done in client code:

```ts
let microphonePermissions = await ExpoSpeechRecognitionModule.getMicrophonePermissionsAsync();

if (!microphonePermissions.granted && microphonePermissions.canAskAgain) {
  microphonePermissions = await ExpoSpeechRecognitionModule.requestMicrophonePermissionsAsync();
}

if (!microphonePermissions.granted) {
  // throw an error
}
```

We are currently experiencing the issue with `ImagePicker.launchCameraAsync` mentioned at the beginning of this thread (camera doesn't open until the app is backgrounded and then resumed). I have a temporary workaround that seems to be working well for us.  

Basically using `setTimeout` to wait 500ms, and then using the `Linking.openURL` function to "re-open" the current app (we have the app SCHEME stored in our environment file because we have a different scheme for each environment).

Snippet of our code:

```typescript
      // this is a temporary workaround for a bug in the expo-image-picker library
      // see: https://github.com/expo/expo/issues/39480
      // the gist of the bug is that due to an Android security update, the camera does not
      // open until the app is backgrounded and then resumed.
      // so for now, we'll simulate that by opening the app's scheme URL after a short delay
      if (Platform.OS === "android") {
        setTimeout(() => {
          Linking.openURL(`${process.env.SCHEME}://`);
        }, 500);
      }

      const imagePickerResult = await ImagePicker.launchCameraAsync({ mediaTypes: ["images"] });
```

it seems that `Camera.getCameraPermissionsAsync()` is always resolving with permissionStatus.
I workedaround the `Camera.requestCameraPermissionsAsync()` not resolving after giving permission like so

```typescript
let hasGranted = false;

// always resolving with status
const {status} = await Camera.getCameraPermissionsAsync();

hasGranted = status === PermissionStatus.GRANTED;

if (status !== PermissionStatus.GRANTED) {
	// resolving only the firstTime
	const {status: askedPermissionStatus} = await Camera.requestCameraPermissionsAsync();
	hasGranted = status === PermissionStatus.GRANTED;
}

// keeps going on depending on hasGranted
```

